## 5. Final Verdict

### 🟢 Will compile and run: **YES**

### 🟢 Will track the rover correctly: **YES** (once GPS + telemetry acquire)

### 🟢 Last_Wish integration: **CORRECT** — servo position saved on power loss

### 🟢 NVS dual-save: **CORRECT** — both systems independent and complementary

### 🔴 Two issues that can cause problems in the field:

| # | Severity | Issue | When it happens |
|---|----------|-------|-----------------|
| 1 | 🔴 **Critical** | Compass I2C failure returns heading=0° silently | Vibration loosens I2C wire, or HMC5883L hangs |
| 2 | 🔴 **Critical** | Bearing computed on (0,0)→(0,0) before GPS acquires | Every boot, until GPS gets first fix (~30-60s) |

### 🟡 Three issues that affect usability:

| # | Severity | Issue |
|---|----------|-------|
| 3 | 🟡 Moderate | Raw telemetry echo (`Serial.print(c)`) floods monitor |
| 4 | 🟡 Moderate | Base station `readStringUntil()` can block for 1 second |
| 5 | 🟢 Minor | Dead commented-out code in `tracking_math.cpp` |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Bottom line:** The system is **functionally correct for normal operation**. The Last_Wish integration is solid. The tracking math, filters, servo control, and data pipeline all work. The two critical issues are **edge-case failure modes** (compass disconnect, pre-GPS-lock boot phase) — they won't cause problems during normal tracking, but they can cause erratic servo behavior during startup or hardware faults.
